// Number values survive reordering as source text, never through an `f64`.
//
// openapi-format cannot do this. It reads documents through a JavaScript object, so a number wider
// than an `f64` would lose digits on the way in. It works around that by rewriting large numbers to
// `<digits>===` sentinel strings before parsing and decoding them again on output, with this regex
// (`utils/file.js:761`):
//
//     /: ([0-9]+(\.[0-9]+)?)\b(?!\.[0-9])===/g
//
// There is no leading `-`, so a negative number is encoded and then never decoded, and the sentinel
// reaches the output as a quoted string. Two files in the benchmark corpora come out corrupt that
// way: `artifacts.yaml` with 192 occurrences of `minimum: -9007199254740991===`, and
// `magic-on-ramps.yaml` with `example: -80.56092644815843===`.
//
// oxfmt has no encode step to get wrong. A numeric value is re-emitted from its source span, so
// every scalar below has to come out byte-identical no matter how many digits it carries. The one
// thing that does rewrite a number here is Prettier's own textual normalization, which is a
// spelling change and never a precision one, so trailing-zero and exponent styles are left to the
// YAML fixture and are not asserted here.
//
// Every schema sits under a `properties` parent, whose table is
// `[description, type, items, format, example, default, enum]` with unranked keys alphabetical
// after it, and each one is written in an order that table changes. So each value is carried
// through the permutation rather than left in place. Nothing inside a schema is commented, because
// a comment in a mapping's span makes it refuse to reorder, which would leave the values sitting
// still and prove nothing. The names carry what each case is for:
//
// - `upstream-corrupts-this` and `-too` hold the two values openapi-format corrupts. Both are
//   negative, so its decoder never matches them.
// - `beyond-exact-integers` needs more than 53 bits. A round-trip through an `f64` returns ...92
//   for `9007199254740993`, so any digit moving here is proof a parse crept in.
// - `beyond-f64-precision` has more significant digits than an `f64` carries. Printing the nearest
//   double gives `0.1`.
// - `overflows-to-infinity` is not finite as an `f64`, and infinity has no JSON number spelling.
// - `sentinel-shaped-string` is a string that already ends in the sentinel. Upstream's decoder
//   rewrites it to a bare number, turning a string into a number. Nothing here reads the sentinel.
{
  "openapi": "3.1.0",
  "info": { "title": "large numbers", "version": "1.0.0" },
  "paths": {},
  "components": {
    "schemas": {
      "Limits": {
        "properties": {
          "upstream-corrupts-this": {
            "default": -9007199254740991,
            "minimum": -9007199254740991,
            "maximum": 9007199254740991,
            "type": "integer"
          },
          "upstream-corrupts-this-too": {
            "example": -80.56092644815843,
            "type": "number"
          },
          "beyond-exact-integers": {
            "default": 9007199254740993,
            "minimum": -9007199254740993,
            "maximum": 18446744073709551616,
            "type": "integer"
          },
          "beyond-f64-precision": {
            "example": 0.1000000000000000055511151231257827,
            "type": "number"
          },
          "overflows-to-infinity": {
            "example": 1e400,
            "default": -1e400,
            "type": "number"
          },
          "sentinel-shaped-string": {
            "example": "9007199254740991===",
            "default": "-80.56092644815843===",
            "type": "string"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
